t1::a A promise type ::t
c2::a "A system outcome, usually a resource configuration, but maybe a message or behaviour." ::c
t3::a "CFEngine employs autonomous agents whose function is to keep certain \"promises\"." ::t
t4::a CFEngine is used for automating the configuration and maintenance of computer systems. ::t
e5::a CFEngine promise type ::e
c6::a CFEngine promises ::c "Some might call promises \"rules\", but they are only self-imposed rules on the agents. · The agents can only promise its own intent to maintain state. If the operating sytem blocks an agent, or some other process interferes then even though it can keep its own promise to try and repair a deviant state, it can't promise that the system will end up according to specification." ::tip
t7::a CFEngine syntax ::t
c8::a "Declarations of \"intended outcome\" or \"desired state\"." ::c
t9::a "For example, the files type promise centers around managing file presence, content, and attributes while vars type promises hold data." ::t
c10::a How autonomous entities called agents can declare their intended behaviour. ::c
t11::a Promise Theory ::t "Promise Theory has nothing a priori to do with \"promises\" in programming languages like Javascript, though programming promise functionality is compatible with the Promise Theory definition.." ::tip
t12::a "Some might call promises \"rules\", but they are only self-imposed rules on the agents." ::t
t13::a Suppose you have 1000 machines of different kinds ... ::t
c14::a The desired state and/or maintainence schedule of an affected system resource / object ::c
t15::a "There was this one time, at LinkedIn camp...." ::t
t16::a Use basic promises rather than embedding shell commands ::t
